00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:12.359
it can get a little bit tricky and a
little complicated, so generally you're

00:02:12.359 --> 00:02:13.679
always given three out of the four.

00:02:14.299 --> 00:02:17.639
So when we are looking at these, we
are going to be exploring all the

00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:21.600
different formulas in many different
problems, so let's take a look at

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.340
how to use these starting with STP.

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.220
Now when it comes to stoichiometry,
the whole idea is that this is

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:36.160
all about unit cancellation.

00:02:36.515 --> 00:02:39.894
So same thing as dimensional
analysis and all the, most of the

00:02:39.894 --> 00:02:41.525
conversions you do in chemistry.

00:02:42.025 --> 00:02:46.535
Now, the thing with stoichiometry
is that generally it uses a

00:02:46.535 --> 00:02:48.215
balanced chemical equation.

00:02:48.744 --> 00:02:51.374
But what we're going to start with
is some problems that don't involve

00:02:51.375 --> 00:02:54.464
a chemical equation, just to get
some of the basics to remind us.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:55.990
of unit cancellation.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.710
So the first problem here is
how many moles of neon are

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.410
in six liters of gas at STP?

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.249
Well, with this problem, you are still
doing unit cancellation, but you're

00:03:06.249 --> 00:03:11.280
involving moles, which is still talking
about how we can read these problems.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:15.590
So the first thing is, if we notice
is that it starts with a given number,

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:20.870
always write down your numeric values,
nine times out of 10, they are important.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.990
So we have six liters.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:27.720
Now, being that this is an STP,
what we can use is, is that

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.309
molar volume, which was 22.

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:33.230
4 liters, is equivalent to one mole.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:37.290
So being that we can use that,
we want to cancel our units.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.459
So being that we have liters
on top, that means we need

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.579
liters also on the bottom here.

00:03:43.029 --> 00:03:44.760
So 22.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:45.519
4 liters.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.609
We now see that, oh, our units are
going to cancel, which is fantastic,

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.470
and that's equivalent to one mole.

00:03:53.929 --> 00:03:56.820
So what does this mean as a little
t table, if this is your first time

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:01.350
seeing these, is you do 6 divided by 22.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.310
4, and you will get your number here.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.299
So what we are going to get is 0.

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:14.190
268 moles, but being that we started
with two significant figures, so you

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:19.160
can see that up here, there's two
digits, We are going to round this to 0.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:20.750
27 moles.

00:04:21.349 --> 00:04:23.159
All right, let's take
a look at the next one.

00:04:23.159 --> 00:04:24.680
So what if I want to do the other way?

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.249
So how many liters would 2.

00:04:27.249 --> 00:04:29.970
4 moles of argon occupy at STP?

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:31.959
Well, same idea, right?

00:04:31.959 --> 00:04:32.250
If I have 2.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.090
4 moles, that's going to be on top.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.950
We will put one mole on the bottom, 22.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:40.080
4 liters on top.

00:04:40.429 --> 00:04:43.729
And what we can once again see
is, hey, mole, mole, cancel.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:44.820
So we now have 2.

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:48.170
4 times 22.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:51.834
4 And as a result, what
we are going to get is 53.

00:04:51.835 --> 00:04:58.334
76 liters, and we are going to once
again round that to two sig figs,

00:04:58.655 --> 00:05:01.614
so we are going to get 54 liters.

00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:05.965
And when we're done, we want to box our
answer off, just like I should have did

00:05:05.965 --> 00:05:10.255
earlier, and that is how you do it with
just how to use the STP conversion.

00:05:10.659 --> 00:05:12.960
Let's take a look at
some chemical equations.

00:05:13.189 --> 00:05:16.419
Now when we work with STP, it
is really important that you

00:05:16.429 --> 00:05:18.479
realize when it is an STP.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.760
Generally it's always listed
in the problem that we have it.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.129
So even though I have the title above,
so you can see it in the problem

00:05:26.129 --> 00:05:28.229
too, so it says calculate the volume.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.420
in liters of ammonia produced when 5.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:37.930
5 liters of H2 react
with excess N2 at STP.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.159
Okay, so we have this thing that says STP.

00:05:41.489 --> 00:05:42.529
That's really important.

00:05:42.539 --> 00:05:44.679
That means your problem
is 10 times easier.

00:05:44.679 --> 00:05:46.689
You do not have to use the ideal gas law.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.810
There's some other key piece
of information too, right?

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:54.305
We were given Uh, the number, we have
the number of liters of hydrogen and

00:05:54.305 --> 00:05:57.645
it tells us we have excess nitrogen,
meaning that doesn't run out.

00:05:57.675 --> 00:05:59.854
So it only has to rely on hydrogen.

00:06:00.224 --> 00:06:04.094
This is not a limiting reactant
problem, which is great, right?

00:06:04.094 --> 00:06:06.215
You'd have to have both
values to have that.

00:06:06.825 --> 00:06:10.555
Now the one thing is if you kind of
notice there's not a chemical equation,

00:06:10.625 --> 00:06:14.805
so what you have to do is, is predict
and balance it, which tends to be a

00:06:14.805 --> 00:06:16.685
really common theme in a lot of these.

00:06:16.685 --> 00:06:16.695
Okay.

00:06:16.695 --> 00:06:16.704
Thanks.

00:06:17.075 --> 00:06:22.244
So one thing it says is how much ammonia
is produced in the problem, right?

00:06:22.244 --> 00:06:24.955
So kind of highlight that
in purple right there.

00:06:25.494 --> 00:06:30.815
So when it says it's produced or made or
created, synthesized, oxidized, all these

00:06:30.815 --> 00:06:36.525
different terms referring to a reaction,
generally that's probably the product.

00:06:36.974 --> 00:06:38.805
So you gotta read it carefully.

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:42.045
Sometimes it depends on
how everything's worded.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.800
But if it says it's made,
that's a sign that, hey, this

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:46.960
has to be on the product side.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:52.380
All right, so now it also says that
hydrogen and ammonia reacted, right?

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.559
So we were given that volume, that excess.

00:06:55.039 --> 00:06:58.109
So let's put those over
here just so we have that.

00:06:58.130 --> 00:06:59.819
So H2 plus N2.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.120
All right, now how do we balance this?

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.695
Well, generally when it comes to
balancing this problem, is you

00:07:05.695 --> 00:07:10.355
notice that we have a odd number of
hydrogens here on the right hand side.

00:07:10.885 --> 00:07:14.945
When you have an odd subscript, this
is something I like to try, always want

00:07:14.945 --> 00:07:17.944
to try first, is even the odds, right?

00:07:17.945 --> 00:07:18.895
So what does that mean?

00:07:18.905 --> 00:07:21.935
Well, we don't like odd
numbers, so let's make it even.

00:07:21.945 --> 00:07:26.325
So let's add a supers, or
coefficient in front of that, of 2.

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:27.474
Now what does that do?

00:07:27.474 --> 00:07:29.865
That makes a total of 6
hydrogens on the right.

00:07:30.385 --> 00:07:35.225
So what we are now going to do is put a
3 in front of the hydrogens on the left.

00:07:35.724 --> 00:07:38.195
Meaning we have six hydrogens
on the left and on the right.

00:07:38.565 --> 00:07:41.075
And one thing you might have
noticed is along with that is

00:07:41.085 --> 00:07:42.635
how many nitrogens you now have.

00:07:43.044 --> 00:07:48.205
Well, you have two nitrogens now on the
left and you already had two on the right.

00:07:48.614 --> 00:07:51.424
So it is balanced and it is happy.

00:07:51.825 --> 00:07:53.185
This is exactly what we want.

00:07:53.325 --> 00:07:53.674
All right.

00:07:54.215 --> 00:07:59.275
So now that we have that, what we're going
to do now is use the piece of information

00:07:59.285 --> 00:08:01.205
that was given to us in the problem.

00:08:01.664 --> 00:08:03.084
Which as you saw, we had 5.

00:08:03.084 --> 00:08:05.725
5 liters of hydrogen.

00:08:06.185 --> 00:08:08.195
And that is at STP.

00:08:08.195 --> 00:08:09.695
So let's kind of write that down.

00:08:10.445 --> 00:08:14.685
So what we are, what we also are
trying to figure out as well is

00:08:14.695 --> 00:08:16.825
how much ammonia was produced.

00:08:17.785 --> 00:08:19.555
We have to use our chemical reaction.

00:08:20.064 --> 00:08:22.525
So the first thing what
we have was that 5.

00:08:22.564 --> 00:08:24.764
5 liters, right, of H2.

00:08:25.125 --> 00:08:27.225
And this is where it's really
important that you're kind of

00:08:27.225 --> 00:08:29.424
writing out all your different units.

00:08:29.935 --> 00:08:33.594
all your species so that way you're
not getting them like mixed up

00:08:33.664 --> 00:08:39.245
when you're showing them because
the molecules have to cancel too.

00:08:39.775 --> 00:08:42.834
So one thing about this is that
you see, okay, if you remember

00:08:42.834 --> 00:08:45.554
our relationship we had 22.

00:08:45.554 --> 00:08:49.345
4 liters is equivalent to
one mole of hydrogen, right?

00:08:49.345 --> 00:08:53.455
So we'll write hydrogen for both
these and we see that liters of

00:08:53.455 --> 00:08:55.815
hydrogen cancels liters of hydrogen.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:59.520
Now that we have moles, right,
your goal is always the mole.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.339
If you can get there, you're
half of the way there.

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:03.589
So, which is fantastic.

00:09:04.219 --> 00:09:06.069
And we want to get to ammonia.

00:09:06.079 --> 00:09:10.620
So, what we now need to do is read
our chemical equation, which you

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:16.620
can see three moles of hydrogen is
equivalent to two moles of ammonia.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.080
So what we are going to do is
we're going to write our three

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.110
moles of hydrogen on the bottom.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:27.080
Reason is, is because now you can
see that, hey, your units now cancel.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:33.715
And now we're going to put two moles
of of ammonia on, or on top, okay?

00:09:34.555 --> 00:09:37.945
And then we're going to finish
it with that, um, that molar

00:09:37.945 --> 00:09:39.255
volume once again, right?

00:09:39.255 --> 00:09:41.154
So we're one mole of NH3.

00:09:41.154 --> 00:09:44.825
Remember any gas is equivalent to 22.

00:09:44.825 --> 00:09:46.845
4 liters of NH3.

00:09:47.664 --> 00:09:51.215
And the last thing is you can
see once again units cancel

00:09:52.195 --> 00:09:53.895
here as well, which is awesome.

00:09:54.830 --> 00:09:58.890
Now the thing about this problem is that
you might notice something if you are

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:03.779
math savvy is that there is more to this
than I can't stop other than the units.

00:10:04.289 --> 00:10:06.769
One thing you'll see is that the 22.

00:10:06.769 --> 00:10:10.935
4 liters down here are on
top here cancels the 22.

00:10:10.935 --> 00:10:14.225
4 liters all the way on
the other side, right?

00:10:14.245 --> 00:10:17.615
There's many different
things that can cancel.

00:10:17.984 --> 00:10:21.404
Same thing with one mole and
one mole all the way across.

00:10:22.044 --> 00:10:25.214
So wait a minute, can this
problem be a lot easier?

00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:27.254
And the answer is absolutely it can.

00:10:27.504 --> 00:10:28.694
So everything's cleaned up.

00:10:28.754 --> 00:10:32.824
Now what we can actually do
is when the problem is at STP,

00:10:33.194 --> 00:10:38.625
you can use the coefficients to
define a liter to liter ratio.

00:10:39.025 --> 00:10:45.424
So meaning that going off our chemical
formula is that two liters of ammonia is

00:10:45.425 --> 00:10:48.634
equivalent to three liters of hydrogen.

00:10:48.999 --> 00:10:51.099
And that's because of
kinetic molecular theory.

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:54.670
All the gases are indiscriminate
particles that you can't tell

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:58.409
the difference between, so there
is a relationship between them.

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:01.010
So you'll get the exact same answer.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.879
So all we have to do is do five and
a half, divide by three, multiply

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:09.780
by two, and what we will get is 3.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:11.719
7 liters of ammonia.

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:17.120
So you can kind of, you can ignore all
that craziness of that long problem.

00:11:17.569 --> 00:11:24.250
So this is when it is at STP that you
can do this step for a volume to volume.

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:28.430
It's nice to know the little tricks
because it can save you a lot of time on

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:30.990
exams that you're limited on your time.

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:32.329
Let's look at another.

00:11:32.699 --> 00:11:34.110
Stoichiometry at STP.

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:37.630
So if you have a chemical equation,
you generally will have to do

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.069
some stoichiometry, meaning that
we're going to have to use the

00:11:40.069 --> 00:11:41.660
mole to mole volume very often.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:49.420
So, with this problem, the metabolic
oxidation of glucose, C6H12O6, in our

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:54.080
bodies produces CO2, which is expelled
through our lungs as a gas, right?

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:54.659
You breathe out.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:59.259
So, if I have, let's say, the following
chemical formula, which is C6H12O6.

00:12:01.505 --> 00:12:06.315
glucose, which is combining with
oxygen to make CO2 and water.

00:12:07.285 --> 00:12:13.805
Calculate the volume of oxygen required
to oxidize 50 grams of glucose at STP.

00:12:14.594 --> 00:12:17.725
Wow, that was a lot of information
in one thing, but it all really

00:12:17.725 --> 00:12:22.395
boils down to just knowing the
chemical equation and this last line.

00:12:22.405 --> 00:12:23.575
So what do we need, right?

00:12:23.575 --> 00:12:28.375
We want the volume and we
started with 50 grams of glucose,

00:12:28.464 --> 00:12:30.625
and that's at STP, which is.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.769
Very, very crucial.

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.030
So, how do we start?

00:12:35.030 --> 00:12:38.100
Well, remember, the goal
is always the mole, right?

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:41.199
How can you get something here to moles?

00:12:41.649 --> 00:12:43.790
Well, you gotta kind of think,
going through your chemist's

00:12:43.839 --> 00:12:45.879
toolbox, what can you convert?

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:49.089
You have pretty much available
to you are three options.

00:12:49.189 --> 00:12:54.885
You have molar mass, the ideal gas law,
or you can essentially use molarity.

00:12:54.955 --> 00:12:58.475
Well, there's no molarity here
and you don't have all of the

00:12:58.475 --> 00:13:00.365
variables for the ideal gas law.

00:13:00.394 --> 00:13:03.195
You have temperature, pressure,
remember you need three out of four.

00:13:03.445 --> 00:13:07.305
You don't have everything, but
you do have the essentially

00:13:07.334 --> 00:13:09.874
glucose here, which is its mass.

00:13:09.874 --> 00:13:13.075
You can always look up the molar
mass via your periodic table.

00:13:13.735 --> 00:13:18.565
So if we are to do that, let's say
if we are to get the molar mass for

00:13:18.565 --> 00:13:24.865
glucose, so meaning you're going to
take six carbons, uh, multiply it by 12.

00:13:24.865 --> 00:13:30.274
011, then you're going to add
that plus 12 hydrogens, each 1.

00:13:30.714 --> 00:13:36.015
008, and then add that by the
six oxygens, you will get 180.

00:13:36.015 --> 00:13:44.025
156 grams of glucose is equivalent
to one mole, okay, of glucose.

00:13:44.945 --> 00:13:47.504
Now, from that, what
can you do from there?

00:13:47.555 --> 00:13:52.210
Well, Being that we are curious about
how much, what, calculate the volume

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:58.120
of oxygen here, we can now do the mole
to mole ratio from the problem, right?

00:13:58.120 --> 00:13:59.780
This is where the
stoichiometry comes from.

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:07.635
You can see that for every six moles
of O2, there is one mole of C6H12O6,

00:14:07.635 --> 00:14:10.035
or glucose, to the equivalent.

00:14:10.035 --> 00:14:18.434
So it's one mole, and we'll put C6H12O6
for every six moles of O2, right?

00:14:18.475 --> 00:14:20.315
They're equivalent to one another.

00:14:21.104 --> 00:14:26.104
So now when that happens you can see
grams cancels grams, our moles of

00:14:26.155 --> 00:14:33.200
glucose cancels our moles of glucose,
And we're left with only our moles of O2.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.250
So we want to check that off.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:35.330
Our unit's all cancelled.

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:36.150
We're good, right?

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:39.640
So if we were to plug this into
our calculator, we'll do 50 times

00:14:39.650 --> 00:14:42.359
6, and then divide that by 180.

00:14:42.389 --> 00:14:43.109
156.

00:14:44.915 --> 00:14:46.815
That will give us how many moles we have.

00:14:46.855 --> 00:14:48.395
Now, is that everything?

00:14:48.405 --> 00:14:49.395
Are we done?

00:14:49.695 --> 00:14:51.325
Well, the thing is, it wants volume.

00:14:51.325 --> 00:14:52.685
So what can I do now?

00:14:53.295 --> 00:14:55.295
Well, if you remember, STP, right?

00:14:55.675 --> 00:14:57.675
So we can do one more step.

00:14:57.785 --> 00:15:02.524
We can use our STP, our molar
volume, to do the last step.

00:15:04.415 --> 00:15:10.464
We got to our goal, but we want to go
one more step, so one mole of O2 is

00:15:10.464 --> 00:15:12.635
now, is going to be equivalent to 22.

00:15:13.155 --> 00:15:15.675
4 liters of gas.

00:15:16.205 --> 00:15:20.225
Alright, now, if you put now
that into your calculator, what

00:15:20.245 --> 00:15:23.145
you are going to get is 37.

00:15:23.234 --> 00:15:28.075
3 liters of oxygen gas
is going to be produced.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:32.440
when you oxidize the 50
grams worth of sugar.

